## Look

This project was generated with the **Nexora** look (`--template nexora`):
frosted-glass panels over a black studio, a glass sidebar, an island top bar,
and a WebGL liquid ribbon behind everything.

| Path | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `src/nexora/nexora.css` | The design system: palette, light and dark tokens, every `nx-*`, `card-panel`, `btn-modern`, `badge-status` class |
| `src/nexora/accent.js` | The accent: presets, deriving the palette from one colour, remembering the viewer's pick |
| `src/nexora/brand.js` | The product name shown on the wordmark |
| `src/nexora/background.js` | Whether the ribbon runs at all: reduced motion, WebGL, lazy loading |
| `src/nexora/liquid.js` | The ribbon itself, on three.js, loaded as its own chunk |
| `src/style.css` | Tailwind, plus the Nexora tokens as utilities: `bg-panel`, `text-ink`, `brand-500` |

**The name** on the sidebar and the sign-in page is `VITE_APP_NAME`, exactly
as written, falling back to `jfastframework` when it is empty. Change it in
`.env` (read by `npm run dev`) and `.env.production` (read by
`npm run build`). `jfast workspace env` rewrites `.env`, so set it again there
after running that.

**The accent** is one colour; glow, deep shade, text-safe shades and the
colour of text on a button are derived from it. Three places set it, first
one wins:

1. the viewer, with the swatch button next to the theme toggle -- six presets
   and a custom colour, kept in this browser;
2. `VITE_ACCENT="#3B82F6"` in `.env` / `.env.production`, the project's
   default -- quoted, because an unquoted `#` starts a comment there;
3. the `--c-*` triplets at the top of `src/nexora/nexora.css`, Nexora's ruby.

Buttons, glows, badges, every `brand-*` utility and the ribbon follow it.

**The ribbon costs nothing when it cannot help.** It is never loaded under
`prefers-reduced-motion`, falls back to a still frame without WebGL 2, and
stops rendering while the tab is hidden.

**Another look** is a generation choice, not a restyle by hand:
`jfast new service <name> --kind spa --frontend {{ frontend }} --template classic`.
`jfast new view` reads the look from `.jfast-template`, so new pages match.

